Micro-RTSP, vendored from https://github.com/geeksville/Micro-RTSP

Vendored rather than pulled through lib_deps because it carries local fixes that
a dependency checkout would silently discard. Only the files this project
actually compiles are here; OV2640Streamer, SimStreamer, JPEGSamples and
platglue-mbed.h are upstream-only and were left out.

Local changes, all in CRtspSession.cpp:

  1. Handle_RtspSETUP reported "destination=127.0.0.1;source=127.0.0.1" while
     CStreamer actually sends RTP to the peer address it reads back from
     socketpeeraddr(). The packets therefore arrived, but any client that
     believes the Transport header discards them. ffmpeg hangs forever on
     "-rtsp_transport udp" for exactly this reason; TCP interleaved was never
     affected, which is what made the failure look intermittent rather than
     total. Now reports the real destination, and omits "source" (optional per
     RFC 2326, and the other platglue headers have no local-address call).

     Measured: UDP went from a hang with zero frames to 175 frames in 14.98s.

  2. The session ID is printed with %u instead of %i. The constructor forces
     bit 31 set, so the signed form emitted tokens like "Session: -2147463434".

  3. Handle_RtspSETUP answers 461 Unsupported Transport for plain UDP, gated on
     -DRTSP_REQUIRE_TCP=1 (set in platformio.ini; build with 0 to allow UDP).

     RTP has no retransmission and this WiFi link stalls for hundreds of
     milliseconds at a time, so whole seconds of video simply vanish. A UDP
     viewer logs "RTP: missed 14-26 packets" over and over, freezes, holds the
     freeze, then drops the session and reconnects. Over TCP interleaved the
     same stalls are only latency.

     Measured back to back: 90s of TCP delivered 502 frames with no errors, and
     130s with the client left to choose its own transport produced no log lines
     at all - it takes the 461 and retries interleaved, which is what RFC 2326
     asks clients to do.

Verified with ffmpeg 'master-latest-win64-gpl-shared' against this board.
